>
> **Second**, I would clean and transform the data using PySpark. This could include handling nulls, removing duplicates, changing data types, applying business rules, and joining data from multiple sources.
>
> **Third**, I would perform data-quality validations before loading the data, such as checking mandatory fields, duplicate records, and invalid values.
>
> **Fourth**, I would configure the JDBC connection to Azure SQL using secure credentials, preferably through a secret-management mechanism rather than hardcoding credentials.
>
> **Fifth**, I would write the transformed data to the required Azure SQL tables using JDBC. Depending on the requirement, I could use append, overwrite, or an upsert/merge strategy.
>
> **Sixth**, I would create a Databricks Job to orchestrate and schedule the pipeline. For example, it could run daily after the source files arrive.
>
> **Finally**, I would add monitoring, logging, error handling, retries, and alerts so that failures can be detected and investigated.
>
> If the pipeline requires intermediate processing or reliable incremental processing, I can use Delta Lake between the raw and transformed stages.”**

